        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Benin Bronzes</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1300 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Edo artists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> Casters, working under royal decree for the obas (kings) of the Kingdom of Benin, who monopolised bronze, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gold</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">Several thousand cast brass and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronze-plaques">bronze plaques</a>, portrait heads, bells, jewellery and smaller pieces, made by lost-wax casting; the rectangular plaques are about 3.2 millimetres thick, and other pieces are of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, wood, ceramic, leather or cloth</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">Largest holdings in the Ethnological Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, with other collections in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and, third largest, in Nigerian museums, principally the Nigerian National Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the palace plaques called Ama; commemorative heads of obas and queen mothers; lost-wax casting; the leopard as the oba's emblem; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-expedition-of-1897">Benin Expedition of 1897</a>; the long dispute over return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a></span></div>
