    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Benin Bronzes decorated the palace of the obas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and honoured the oba, the divine king, and the queen mother; only the king could own objects of bronze and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and he could allow high-ranking people to use some of them.</li><li>Most of the plaques and other objects were taken by British forces during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-expedition-of-1897">Benin Expedition of 1897</a>, and were then divided among museums and private collections in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and elsewhere.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> has asked for their return since independence in 1960, and from 2021 museums and governments began to transfer ownership, while the largest British collection stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Late 19th-century scholars concluded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> metalworking came from Portuguese traders, a conclusion now held to be mistaken, since bronzes were made in Benin before the Portuguese arrived.</p></aside>
    </section>

