    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">13th century</span><span class="rf-text">By tradition, the son of the Oni of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> metal workers the art of casting bronze by the lost-wax technique</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">14th century</span><span class="rf-text">Edo artists began to produce the sculptures, some of which date to this century</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">15th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> began to trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and slaves with the Portuguese and used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manilla">manillas</a> bought from them as a metal source</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1550</span><span class="rf-text">The first of two golden ages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> metalwork is believed to have come in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Oba Esigie</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1735</span><span class="rf-text">The second golden age is believed to have come in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eresoyen">Eresoyen</a>, from 1735 to 1750</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1897</span><span class="rf-text">British forces sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> and took the works of art decorating the royal palace and the nobility's residences</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1950</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> began to sell more than 30 Benin Bronzes to the Nigerian government, sales that stopped in 1972</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2022</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> signed a political declaration to return 1,130 bronzes, and the Horniman Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> transferred its Benin Bronzes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/esigie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esigie.jpg" alt="Esigie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Esigie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MARKK_C2897_Oba_with_two_dignitaries_DSC-1074_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">of Bronze Casters Guild Igun Eronmwon, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Kingdom of Benin</a> occupied southern parts of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> between the fourteenth and nineteenth centuries and was rich in sculptures of iron, bronze, wood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and terracotta. The Benin Bronzes are several thousand metal plaques and sculptures that decorated the royal palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>, made by artists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-people">Edo people</a> from the fourteenth century. Most of the collection dates to the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, and it is believed that two golden ages of Benin metalwork fell in the reigns of the obas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Esigie</a> (fl. 1550) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eresoyen">Eresoyen</a> (1735–1750).</p><p>The Benin bronze tradition is thought to have derived from or been influenced by that of the older Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> in south-west Nigeria. Tradition holds that the son of the Oni, the sovereign of Ife, taught Benin metal workers lost-wax casting in the thirteenth century, and in 1939 heads very like Benin's were found at Ife, dated to the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries. Late 19th-century scholars O. M. Dalton and Charles Hercules Read concluded that Benin metallurgy came from the Portuguese, an error, since bronzes were made in Benin before the Portuguese came.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Benin Bronzes&#8221; cites where it covers Social context and creation:</p><ul><li>Kate Ezra, Royal art of Benin: the Perls collection in the Metropolitan Museum of Art (The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1992)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The plaques, called Ama in the Edo language, depict scenes or themes from the history of the kingdom. They exist in two formats: one with turned-back vertical edges decorated with an incised guilloché pattern, and a narrower one without them. Most fronts are incised with foliate patterns of one to four leaves, called ebe-ame, the river leaf design; the leaves were used in healing rites by priestesses of Olokun, the god of the sea. Some reliefs show battles of the sixteenth-century wars of expansion, but most show dignitaries in ceremonial dress, usually static figures arranged around a central one. Many can be identified only by their clothing and emblems, which indicated rank and function, and attempts to link them with historical figures have been speculative and unverified.</p><p>The bronze heads were made for ancestral altars and held carved elephant tusks in openings in the top. Heads of the king or queen mother are archetypes, not individual portraits, and their style changed over the centuries. The leopard, which symbolises the oba, recurs in many pieces, as does the royal triad of the oba flanked by two assistants. Although called bronzes, most pieces are brass, an alloy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, zinc and lead in various proportions.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Benin Bronzes&#8221; cites where it covers Themes:</p><ul><li>Philip Dark, An Introduction to Benin Art and Technology (Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1973)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The palace was the site of production of the royal ancestral altars and of an elabourate court ceremonial life. Artists working in bronze were organised into a kind of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> under royal decree and lived in a special area of the palace under the oba's direct control. The oba monopolised the materials hardest to obtain, such as gold, elephant tusks and bronze. The figures were cast in relief by the lost-wax technique, with details incised in the wax model, and the plaques were hung on the palace pillars by nails punched through them. The quality of the work was higher when the king was especially powerful and could employ many specialists, and casters reached plaques only about 3 millimetres thick.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> began to trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and slaves with the Portuguese in the late fifteenth century and used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manilla">manillas</a>, brass ingots in the form of bracelets bought from them, as a metal source. Their brass, once suggested to come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, is now thought to come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. According to some sources, Benin artists may have been inspired by items the Portuguese brought, but Babatunde Lawal cites relief carving in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> to argue that the plaques are indigenous to Benin.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Benin Bronzes&#8221; cites where it covers Social context and creation, Technique:</p><ul><li>Charlotta Dohlvik, Museums and Their Voices: A Contemporary Study of the Benin Bronzes (International Museum Studies, 2006)</li><li>Kate Ezra, Royal art of Benin: the Perls collection in the Metropolitan Museum of Art (The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1992)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As a courtly art, the objects aimed to glorify the oba and the history of his power, or to honour the Iyoba, the queen mother. When a king died, his successor ordered a bronze head of his predecessor, and installing an altar for the predecessor was a prerequisite for succession. In the annual festivals to reinforce the mystical power of the oba, the new king made offerings at these altars, held to be essential to the continuation of his reign. Approximately 170 of these heads survive, and their stylistic variation is the main scientific basis for a chronology.</p><p>Few African works had been collected by Europeans before the nineteenth century, and when more were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> they were described as curiosities of pagan cults. That attitude changed after 1897. The Benin Bronzes are more naturalistic than most African art of their period, and the lost-wax casting is considered among the best of African sculpture. A sixteenth-century bronze showing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> with Europeans featured in the BBC and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> series A History of the World in 100 Objects, begun in 2010. Ownership of bronze and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> was reserved to the king, who could allow high-ranking individuals to use items such as hanging masks and cuffs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harry-rawson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-rawson.jpg" alt="Harry Rawson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harry-rawson">Harry Rawson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Admiral_Sir_Harry_Rawson,_Sydney,_c._1902.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Freeman Brothers Studio, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In January 1897 a party led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-robert-phillips">James Robert Phillips</a> of the Niger Coast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> set out toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> with six other British officials, two businessmen, translators and 215 porters. Its purpose is disputed: the stated aim was to negotiate with the oba, while some historians hold that it was a reconnaissance disguised as diplomacy, aimed at overthrowing him. The survivors said they were ambushed by the oba's warriors, and only two Europeans survived. A naval punitive expedition under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-rawson">Harry Rawson</a> sacked and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> and took the works decorating the palace and the nobles' residences. The British account held that the attack answered an ambush of a peaceful mission and freed the population from a reign of terror. A 2020 book notes that since the 1960s historians have increasingly seen the expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovonramwen">Oba Ovonramwen</a> as dictated by long-standing policy, not as retaliation.</p><p>Some pieces went to British officials, the Foreign Office sold many, mostly to German and American museums, and it gave a large number of plaques to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, which holds about 900 objects. By 1988, perhaps as few as fifty pieces remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> while about 2,400 were in European and American collections. Dan Hicks gives a total of up to 10,000 looted objects.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Benin Bronzes&#8221; cites where it covers European interest and the Benin Expedition of 1897:</p><ul><li>Janette Greenfield, The Return of Cultural Treasures (Cambridge University Press, 2007)</li><li>Charlotta Dohlvik, Museums and Their Voices: A Contemporary Study of the Benin Bronzes (International Museum Studies, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/elgin-marbles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elgin-marbles.jpg" alt="Elgin Marbles" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/elgin-marbles">Elgin Marbles</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elgin-marbles-jan-2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jay.M, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Since independence in 1960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> has sought the return of the bronzes, a case regarded as a test of the debate over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a> of cultural objects from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, comparable to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elgin-marbles">Elgin Marbles</a>. From 1950 to 1972 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> sold more than 30 pieces to Nigeria as duplicates. In July 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> signed a declaration to return 1,130 bronzes, and a first 20 were handed over in Abuja that December. The Horniman Museum, the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>, Jesus College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, the Metropolitan Museum of Art and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smithsonian">Smithsonian</a> Institution also returned or transferred objects, and in 2025 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> returned 119. The British Museum's collection remains protected by an act of Parliament.</p><p>The returns have raised questions of ownership. In March 2023 President Muhammadu Buhari decreed that all repatriated objects belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> Royal Family, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-ii">Ewuare II</a> announced plans for a museum near his palace. Brigitta Hauser-Schäublin called Germany's 2022 restitution policy a fiasco. In 2022 the Restitution Study Group, an African-American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> group, petitioned against returns, arguing that descendants of enslaved Africans should have co-ownership; the petition was denied. The Digital Benin database, launched in November 2022, lists over 5,000 artefacts held by more than 130 institutions in 20 countries.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Benin Bronzes are several thousand plaques and sculptures made by Edo artists from the fourteenth century for the palace of the obas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. Most were taken by British forces in 1897 and scattered among museums, and since 2021 some have been returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</em></p>

